From: Codex Executor <codex-executor@local>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:39:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 22/24] sheet route: root-cause the sliver artefact of the
 label operator (needle cap triangles from forced support points)

lambda_max of the fixed-port operator was up to 11x its converged value in a quarter of the production labels, its
eigenvector sitting on the four corners of one carrier square: a sliver tetrahedron, not a port stiffness.  Every
sub-degree tetrahedron stands on a needle cap triangle whose apex is a carrier support point forced into a shallow
intrusion of the band into a carrier triangle, a hair from the chain; Triangle may not split the chain and the flip
pass protects it, and a tetrahedron on a needle base has shape-function gradients of 1/width whatever its height.

Fix, in the surface stage: no support point where the band is shallower than 0.05 in level units; support points are
never feature-size snap targets; a needle whose apex or long-edge endpoint is a cap-owned vertex is collapsed (2D link
condition, orientation, no duplicates) or its apex moved into the fan.  Receipt: surface.cap_needles and the
localisation of the stiffest mode (schur.top_mode_mass_on_4_nodes).  Production-tier labels now sit within 1-5 % of
the reference's lambda_max (was up to 2x / 16x) with the top mode spread over 10-11 nodes.

Rejected with evidence: CGAL tetrahedral remeshing with the boundary locked (cpp/pred777h_sliver_remesh: cannot flip
next to a locked boundary; Mesh_3 exude/perturb cannot take our boundary, and a Triangulation_3 cannot hold a
genus-5 solid alone) and a boundary-locked sliver perturbation (kept opt-in: it trades harmless slivers for flat
tetrahedra on the port face and raised lambda_max/reference from 1.01 to 1.13).

Also: the null-space section of the cut-carrier report (dim Null(S) = 3 dim null(prolongation) + 3 x components,
proven; the rank is mesh dependent while q is not; decisions of record), the diagnostics behind both findings, and
the skin validation's restored service-load dictionary.

